# RouteRoom 3D needs one environment variable: a public Mapbox access
# token for the Mapbox GL JS v3 / Mapbox Standard visual provider (see
# ADR 0007, docs/adr/0007-mapbox-standard-visual-provider.md).
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN=pk.your-public-token-here
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAPBOX_BROWSER_MONTHLY_LOAD_CAP=100
#
# Rules for this token:
# - It must be a public token, the kind that starts with "pk.". A secret
#   token ("sk.") is rejected by the app at startup; never put one here.
# - In your Mapbox account, restrict the token by URL to localhost during
#   development and to your deployed domain(s) in production.
# - Never commit a real token. Put it in a real `.env.local` file (already
#   gitignored), not in this file.
# - The app never logs this token and never proxies it through a backend;
#   it is read directly by the browser, which is what a public token is for.
# - The optional browser load cap is a best-effort brake on accidental local
#   refresh loops. It is not a global account cap and cannot prevent overage.
# - Without a valid token (or without WebGL), the planner falls back to the
#   SVG map with a short notice. Nothing else in the product depends on it.
#
# Beyond this one variable, RouteRoom 3D has no database and no other
# server-side API keys. If you add a real API integration later (routing,
# geocoding, weather), put its keys in `.env.local` and document the
# variable name here as a comment only. Never commit an actual key, and
# never put one in a WebMCP tool's input or output schema.
